<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.3.3: Solve by Completing the Square</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02363</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.3.3: Solve by Completing the Square</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3f7422ba-0247-4ced-aeb3-1792ec3a21f5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (10 minutes)</title>
<para>In this activity, students analyze worked examples of equations solved by completing the square. The work allows them
  to further develop their understanding of the method when used with rational numbers and to notice common errors.
  Identifying errors in worked examples is an opportunity to attend to precision and to analyze and critique the
  reasoning of others.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Display the four equations in the task statement for all to see:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \(x^2+14x=-24\) </item>
  <item> \(x^2−10x+16=0\) </item>
  <item> \(x^2+2.4x=-0.8\) </item>
  <item> \(x^2−\frac{6}{5}x+\frac{1}{5}=0\) </item>
</list>
<para>Arrange students in groups of three to four. Assign one equation (or more, depending on time constraints) for each
  group to solve by completing the square. Once group members agree on the solutions, ask them to look for errors in the
  worked solution for the same equation (as the one they solved).</para>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for English Language Learners<title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Speaking, Representing<title>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Speaking, Representing</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">

    <para>Give students additional time to make sure that everyone in their group can explain the errors they identified and proposed corrections. Invite groups to rehearse what they will say when they share with the whole class. Rehearsing provides students with additional opportunities to speak and clarify their thinking and will improve the quality of explanations shared during the whole-class discussion. Make sure to vary who is selected to represent the work of the group so that students get accustomed to preparing each other to fill that role.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Support sense-making; Cultivate conversation</para>
  </div></div>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for Students with Disabilities<title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title></para>
  <para>Engagement: Develop Effort and Persistence<title>Engagement: Develop Effort and Persistence</title></para>
   </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>Chunk this task into manageable parts for students who benefit from support with organizational skills in problem solving. Check in with students after the first two to three minutes of work time. Invite one to two students to share how they identified the error in the first worked solution. Record their thinking on a display for all to see, and keep the work visible as students continue.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Supports accessibility for: Organization; Attention</para>
  </div>
</div>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Here are four equations followed by worked solutions of the equations. Each solution has at least one error.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve the equation \(x^2+14x=-24\). Then look at the following worked solution (with errors) and find and describe
    the error or errors in the worked solution.</item>
</list>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rlr}x^{2}+14 x &amp; =-24 &amp; \text { Step 1 } \\ x^{2}+14 x+28 &amp; =4 &amp; \text { Step 2 } \\
  (x+7)^{2} &amp; =4 &amp; \text { Step 3 } \\ x + 7 &amp; =2 &amp; \text { Step 4 } \\ x+7 &amp; =-2 &amp; \text { Step
  5 } \\ x &amp; =-5 \text { and } x=9 &amp; \text { Step 6 } \\ \end{array} \) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What are the solutions you calculated for \(x^2 + 14x = −24\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=-2\) and \(x=-12\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated" start="2">
  <item>Where was the error made in the worked solution above and what was the error? </item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer: </para>
<para>Error: In Step 2, the number being added, 28, is not a perfect square. It should be 49.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve the equation \(x^2−10x+16=0\). Then look at the following worked solution (with errors) and find and
    describe the error or errors in the worked solution.</item>
</list>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rlr} x^{2}-10 x+16 &amp; =0 &amp; \text { Step 1 } \\ x^{2}-10 x+25 &amp; =9 &amp; \text { Step 2 }
  \\ (x-5)^{2} &amp; =9 &amp; \text { Step 3 } \\ x-5 &amp; =9 &amp; \text { Step 4 } \\ x-5 &amp; =-9 &amp; \text {
  Step 5 } \\ x=14 &amp; \text { and } x=-4 &amp; \text { Step 6 } \end{array} \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=8\) and \(x=2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Where was the error made in the worked solution above and what was the error? </item>
</list>
<para><span class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</span></para>
<para>Errors: Step 4 should have 3 on the right side, not 9. Step 5 should have -3 on the right side, not -9.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve the equation \(x^2+2.4x=-0.8\). Then look at the following worked solution (with errors) and find and
    describe the error or errors in the worked solution.</item>
</list>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rlr}x^2+2.4x=-0.8\;\;\text{ Step 1}&amp; \\ x^2+2.4x+1.44=0.64\quad\text{Step 2}&amp; \\
  (x+1.2)^2=0.64\quad\text{Step 3}&amp; \\ x+1.2=0.8\quad\text{Step 4}&amp; \\ x=-0.4\quad\text{Step 5}&amp;\end{array}
  \) </para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=-0.4\) and \(x=-2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Where was the error made in the worked solution above and what was the error? </item>
</list>
<para><span class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer: </span></para>
<para>Error: In Step 4, only the positive square root is written and the negative square root is neglected.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve the equation \(x^2−\frac{6}{5}x+\frac{1}{5}=0\). Then look at the following worked solution (with
    errors) and find and describe the error or errors in the worked solution.</item>
</list>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rlr}x^2-\frac65x+\frac15&amp;=0&amp;\text{Step
  1}\\x^2-\frac65x+\frac9{25}&amp;=\frac9{25}&amp;\text{Step 2}\\\left(x-\frac35\right)^2&amp;=\frac9{25}&amp;\text{Step
  3}\\x-\frac35&amp;=\frac35&amp;\text{Step 4}\\x-\frac35&amp;=-\frac35&amp;\text{Step
  5}\\x=\frac65&amp;\text{ and }x=0&amp;\text{Step 6}\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x=1\) and \(x=\frac{1}{5}\)</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Where was the error made in the worked solution above and what was the error? </item>
</list>
<para><span class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer: </span></para>
<para>Errors: For example:
  </para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>To involve more students in the discussion, after each student presents, consider asking students to classify each
  error by type (not limited to one type per error) and explain their classification. Here are some examples of types of
  errors:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> Careless errors—for example, writing the wrong number or symbol, repeating the same step twice, leaving out
    a negative sign, or not following directions. </item>
  <item> Computational errors—for example, adding or subtracting incorrectly or putting a decimal point in the wrong
    place. </item>
  <item> Gaps in understanding—for example, misunderstanding of the problem or the rules of algebra, applying an
    ineffective strategy, or choosing the wrong operation or step. </item>
  <item> Lack of precision or incomplete communication—for example, missing steps or explanations, incorrect
    notation, or forgetting labels or parentheses. </item>
</list>
<title>9.3.3: Self Check </title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their
    understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Examine the work shown. Which of the following is the error that took place when the equation was solved by
  completing the square?</para>
<para>\(x^2-6x-7=0\)</para>
<para>\(x^2-6x=7\)</para>
<para>\(x^2-6x+9=16\)</para>
<para>\((x-3)^2=16\)</para>
<para>\(x-3=4\)</para>
<para>\(x=7\)</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        The 7 should have been subtracted from each side.
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: The inverse operation was correctly used and 7 was added to both
        sides. The correct answer is: When taking the square root, only the positive solution was included.
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        When taking the square root, only the positive solution was included.
      </entry>
      <entry>
        That's correct! Check yourself: When taking \(\sqrt{(x-3)^2}=\sqrt{16}\) the positive and negative 4s must be
        considered so that \(x-3= \pm \; 4\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        When completing the square, the wrong number was found for \((\frac{b}{2})^2\).
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: \((\frac{6}{2})^2=9\) so the square was completed correctly. The
        correct answer is when taking the square root, only the positive solution was included.
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        This equation cannot be solved by completing the square.
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let's try again a different way: This problem can be solved by completing the square since it is a
        quadratic equation with a real coefficient of \(x\). The correct answer is: When taking the square root, only
        the positive solution was included.
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>9.3.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on
    their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish
    to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Spotting the Error When Solving Quadratics</title>
<para>To find the error when solving quadratics, it is helpful to first solve the problem on your own.</para>
<para>Solve the quadratic equation by completing the square and finding its zeros. Identify the error in the worked
  solution below.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{ll} x^{2}+10 x-34=0 &amp; \text { Step 1 } \\ x^{2}+10 x=34 &amp; \text { Step 2 } \\ x^{2}+10
  x+25=34-25 &amp; \text { Step 3 } \\ (x+5)^{2}=9 &amp; \text { Step 4 } \\ x+5=\pm \; 3 &amp; \text { Step 5 } \\
  x=-8, x=-2 &amp; \text { Step 6 } \end{array} \)</para>
<para>Now, find the error by working through the problem and comparing steps:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{ll} x^{2}+10 x-34=0 &amp; \text { Step 1 } \\ x^{2}+10 x=34 &amp; \text { Step 2 } \\ x^{2}+10
  x+25=34+25 &amp; \text { Step 3 } \end{array} \)</para>
<para>In Step 3, 25 was added to the left side of the equation, but 25 was SUBTRACTED from the right side of the equation.
  Equations must remain balanced, and 25 should be added to each side of the equation.</para>
<title>Try It: Spotting the Error When Solving Quadratics</title>
<para>Examine the steps below and identify the error in solving the quadratic equation by completing the square:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{lcc}x^2+8x+7=0&amp;\text{ Step 1 }&amp; \\ x^2+8x=-7&amp;\text{ Step 2 }&amp; \\
  x^2+8x+16=-7+16&amp;\text{ Step 3 }&amp; \\ (x+4)^2=9&amp;\text{ Step 4 }&amp; \\
  x+4=3&amp;\text{ Step 5 }&amp; \\ x=-1&amp;\text{ Step 6 }&amp;\end{array} \)</para>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to find the error when completing the square:</para>
<para>Work through the steps on your own and compare:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{lc}x^2+8x+7=0&amp;\text{ Step 1 } \\ x^2+8x=-7&amp;\text{ Step 2 } \\
  x^2+8x+16=-7+16&amp;\text{ Step 3 } \\ (x+4)^2=9&amp;\text{ Step 4 } \\
  x+4=\pm3&amp;\text{ Step 5 } \\ x=-1,x=-7&amp;\text{ Step 6 }\end{array}\)</para>
<para>The error occurred in Step 5 when the plus-minus sign was not included after taking the square root of each side.
  This led to the second possible equation not being written.</para></section>
</content>
